setpriv: check that --seccomp-filter files are executable

From: Skye Soss <skye@soss.website>

A seccomp filter decides what every system call made by the program
returns, including the prctl(PR_GET_SECUREBITS) and execveat() calls
that exec-aware programs rely on. A filter that makes either of them
fail or return 0 turns off their checks, so loading one is running code.

Check the filter file with AT_EXECVE_CHECK and refuse to load it when
the check fails and EXEC_RESTRICT_FILE is set. An administrator can
still allow a filter by giving it execute permission.

The check uses the securebits setpriv was started with, since the
filter is loaded before --securebits is applied.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 sys-utils/setpriv.1.adoc |    2 ++
 sys-utils/setpriv.c      |   16 ++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sys-utils/setpriv.1.adoc b/sys-utils/setpriv.1.adoc
index e67a23454..30160da0c 100644
--- a/sys-utils/setpriv.1.adoc
+++ b/sys-utils/setpriv.1.adoc
@@ -135,6 +135,8 @@ For example grant file read access to everything under */boot*:
 Load raw BPF seccomp filter code from a file.
 +
 Filters can for example be created with *enosys*.
++
+A filter can change the result of any system call made by _program_, so it is treated as code: if the *exec_restrict_file* securebit is set, the file must pass the executability check described in *execveat*(2) under *AT_EXECVE_CHECK*, which usually means it must have execute permission. The check uses the securebits *setpriv* was started with, not the ones set with *--securebits*.
 
 *--reset-env*::
 * clears all the environment variables except *TERM* environment variable
diff --git a/sys-utils/setpriv.c b/sys-utils/setpriv.c
index 9ec3ed96a..99d98a5dc 100644
--- a/sys-utils/setpriv.c
+++ b/sys-utils/setpriv.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <sys/prctl.h>
+#include <sys/syscall.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <paths.h>
@@ -72,6 +73,9 @@
 #ifndef SECBIT_EXEC_DENY_INTERACTIVE_LOCKED
 # define SECBIT_EXEC_DENY_INTERACTIVE_LOCKED (1 << 11)
 #endif
+#ifndef AT_EXECVE_CHECK
+# define AT_EXECVE_CHECK 0x10000
+#endif
 
 #define SETPRIV_EXIT_PRIVERR 127	/* how we exit when we fail to set privs */
 
@@ -742,6 +746,18 @@ static void do_seccomp_filter(const char *file)
 		err(SETPRIV_EXIT_PRIVERR,
 		    _("cannot open %s"), file);
 
+	/* The filter decides what every syscall returns, including the
+	 * prctl() and execveat() calls used by exec-aware programs, so it
+	 * is code and must be executable like any other. */
+	if (syscall(SYS_execveat, fd, "", (char *const[]){ (char *) file, NULL },
+		    NULL, AT_EMPTY_PATH | AT_EXECVE_CHECK) != 0) {
+		int bits = prctl(PR_GET_SECUREBITS, 0, 0, 0, 0);
+
+		if (bits >= 0 && (bits & SECBIT_EXEC_RESTRICT_FILE))
+			errx(SETPRIV_EXIT_PRIVERR,
+			     _("cannot load %s: blocked by EXEC_RESTRICT_FILE"), file);
+	}
+
 	s = read_all_alloc(fd, &filter);
 	if (s < 0)
 		err(SETPRIV_EXIT_PRIVERR,
